 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-meaning"><h3>What is Zakat al-Fitr and why is it given?</h3>
 <p>Zakat al-Fitr, also called Fitrana or Sadaqat al-Fitr, is charity associated with the close of Ramadan and the arrival of Eid al-Fitr. It is distinct from the yearly Zakat al-Mal on wealth and from compensations connected to missed fasts. Its purpose includes providing for people in need at Eid. Ibn Abbas's narration describes purification and feeding the poor in <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:1609" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 1609</a>.</p>
 <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1503" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1503</a> reports Ibn Umar describing the prescribed food amount for Muslims, young and old, male and female, and the timing before Eid prayer. The wording concerns food, not a timeless price in pounds. Food prices, currencies and delivery choices change.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-people"><h3>Who should be included in a household Fitrana calculation?</h3>
 <p>First establish the financial conditions and on whose behalf you are responsible under the school you follow. The hadith includes children; a parent or guardian may make arrangements on their behalf. Other dependency and family circumstances require clarification. Some scholars require a surplus of food over the Eid day and night, while Hanafi discussions use a Nisab-related test. The application deliberately does not turn household size into a religious verdict.</p>
 <p>The calculator splits the count into adults and children so you can review the total without collecting names, ages or family profiles. The rate applies to the combined count you enter. A parent with two children can check three people without pretending the calculator knows whether they are all legally included.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-food"><h3>One sa' of food, wheat and dates: why rates can differ</h3>
 <p>The traditional measure is a <strong>sa'</strong>, a measure of volume associated with staple food. A kilogram equivalent differs by the food's density, so the source narration should not be converted into a universal precise weight for every food. Charities may publish monetary equivalents for local food packages, wheat, dates, rice or particular distribution arrangements.</p>
 <p>Some scholars allow giving a monetary equivalent, while others emphasise delivering food. A charity receiving money as an agent to purchase and distribute food is also a question of permissible arrangements and timing. For a practical UK example of how one organisation calculates a current figure, consult <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/ramadan/zakat-al-fitr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's Zakat al-Fitr explanation</a>. We do <strong>not</strong> make that charity's current rate the automatic value for every reader.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-calculation"><h3>How to use the Fitrana calculator step by step</h3>
 <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li>Confirm who is included and whether the obligation applies to your household under relevant guidance.</li><li>Check the food or cash method your mosque or scholar accepts, and obtain an up-to-date rate from the recipient charity or provider.</li><li>Choose a currency and enter the adults, children and verified per-person rate. Changing currency clears monetary amounts rather than silently converting them.</li><li>If useful, enter a different charity's documented rate purely for comparison; do not combine the two into one price.</li><li>Keep optional voluntary Sadaqah separate from the calculated Fitrana subtotal.</li><li>Download or print a conditional estimate and arrange payment before the appropriate local delivery deadline.</li></ol>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-example"><h3>Worked example: Fitrana for a family of four</h3>
 <div class="three-tool-example"><p><strong>Hypothetical rates only:</strong> Two adults and two children give <strong>4 people</strong>. At an invented <strong>£5 per person</strong>, the Fitrana subtotal is <strong>4 × £5 = £20</strong>. Adding an optional <strong>£2 voluntary donation</strong> makes £22 to arrange, but the Fitrana component remains £20.</p><p>If an alternative valid local provider quoted an illustrative £7 per person, its <strong>4 × £7 = £28</strong> subtotal would be a different estimate. This comparison is neither a current market quote nor a religious recommendation of one method over another.</p></div>
 <p>For a different household or rate, use the calculator above. If your local authority treats a specific dependent differently, adjust the count only after obtaining guidance.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-deadline"><h3>When should Fitrana be paid and distributed?</h3>
 <p>The hadith's time instruction is <strong>before people go out to the Eid prayer</strong>. A transfer just before the prayer may not reach the poor on time if an intermediary needs processing time. Ask when your receiving organisation stops accepting payments for that Ramadan's distribution and how it meets the religious timing requirements.</p>
 <p>The Eid date is subject to local moon-sighting decisions. Our <a href="/tools/ramadan-timetable/">Ramadan Timetable</a> helps plan illustrative dates, but it is not a substitute for an announced Eid or your local prayer time. If you have already missed a deadline, consult qualified guidance rather than assuming the calculator settles the status of a late payment.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fitrana-differences"><h3>Fitrana versus Fidya, Kaffarah and annual Zakat</h3>
 <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Fitrana</h4><p>End-of-Ramadan giving linked to people and the Eid deadline, with a prescribed food basis.</p></div><div><h4>Fidya</h4><p>Specific compensatory feeding in eligible fasting circumstances. See the <a href="/tools/fidya-calculator/">Fidya guide</a>.</p></div><div><h4>Kaffarah</h4><p>Expiation rules for particular cases such as an oath or specific Ramadan violations. See <a href="/tools/kaffarah-calculator/">Kaffarah</a>.</p></div><div><h4>Zakat al-Mal</h4><p>Annual qualifying-wealth assessment involving Nisab and hawl. See the <a href="/tools/zakat-calculator/">Zakat Calculator</a>.</p></div></div>
 </section>
